20:56:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really old and intuitive idea—the "brawn vs. brains" trade-off. It shows up in myths, in movies, in the way people talk. The big strong guy isn't the clever one. The scrawny kid is the genius. Nature only gives you so much, and you either get one or the other. So I get why it feels natural. It feels balanced. Almost fair. The football player got the body, so of course he didn't get the mind. But here's the uncomfortable thing: that trade-off isn't actually rooted in biology. It's a story we tell, not a rule nature follows. Physically, there's no inverse relationship between muscle mass and intelligence. If anything, the opposite is true—exercise, including intense physical training, improves brain function, neurogenesis, and cognitive performance. The human body doesn't have a fixed "stat points" system like a video game where putting points into strength means taking them out of intelligence. That's not how genetics or development works. A person can be both strong and smart. The two traits aren't competing for a single resource. What *is* a zero-sum game is time. And that's where the story comes from. If a kid spends four hours a day at football practice, that's four hours not spent reading, studying, or doing academic enrichment. The trade-off is real in terms of hours, not in terms of inherent capacity. That's a practical constraint, not a biological destiny. And then culture amplifies it. Once a group is labeled "the brawn ones," they get less encouragement, lower expectations, and a ready-made identity to fall into. The prophecy fulfills itself, and it looks like nature when it's really nurture. So no, I don't think football players got "dealt the brawn hand" in some cosmic allocation that left them short on brains. I think many of them were channeled into a system that prioritized one and neglected the other, and then people looked at the result and said "that's just nature." Your cousin doesn't have to be a data point for that story. But I understand why the story feels true—it's been told for so long it sounds like common sense.
